---
import ComparisonLayout from '../../../layouts/ComparisonLayout.astro';
---

<ComparisonLayout
  slug="mureka-vs-minimax"
  a="mureka"
  b="minimax"
  title="Mureka vs MiniMax Music 3: Toolkit or Open Weights | Sonura"
  description="Mureka O2 vs MiniMax Music 3.0 on stems, MIDI, fine-tuning, open weights, and audio spec. Verified August 2026, for anyone building on top of a music model."
  keywords="mureka vs minimax, minimax vs mureka, mureka o2, minimax music 3, ai music api, music model fine tuning, stem separation api, open weights music model"
  lede="two very different answers to the same question. mureka gives you a finished toolkit behind an api: stems, midi, region edits, fine-tuning. minimax gives you the checkpoint and nothing else. one saves you six months, the other saves you a vendor."
  verdict={[
    {
      label: 'Best toolkit',
      pick: 'mureka',
      body: '5-stem and 12-stem separation, midi export, region editing, extend, remix, and a fine-tuning api, all ready to call.',
    },
    {
      label: 'Best to own',
      pick: 'minimax',
      body: 'open weights, five-minute songs in one pass, and a pipeline that no pricing page or deprecation notice can touch.',
    },
  ]}
  headToHead={[
    {
      dimension: 'Stems',
      a: '5-stem and 12-stem separation on any track, at quality comparable to dedicated separation tools.',
      b: 'none at all. minimax returns a finished mix and that is the end of the pipeline.',
      winner: 'a',
    },
    {
      dimension: 'MIDI',
      a: 'exports midi alongside audio, so parts arrive as notes you can rewrite rather than audio you can only cut.',
      b: 'no midi. audio out, and nothing structural to work with.',
      winner: 'a',
    },
    {
      dimension: 'Owning the model',
      a: 'closed. you are calling someone else\'s service, with everything that implies about pricing and continuity.',
      b: 'open checkpoint on hugging face. it is on your disk and it stays there.',
      winner: 'b',
    },
    {
      dimension: 'Custom models',
      a: 'a fine-tuning api, so a house sound can be trained, versioned, and called from your own code.',
      b: 'you have the weights, so you can fine-tune however you like, and you are building that pipeline yourself.',
      winner: 'tie',
    },
    {
      dimension: 'Length in one pass',
      a: 'full songs, with extend and region edit filling in whatever the base generation does not cover.',
      b: 'five minutes in a single coherent pass, holding vocal identity and arrangement all the way through.',
      winner: 'b',
    },
    {
      dimension: 'Audio spec',
      a: 'production-ready audio plus midi, with no extra post-processing stage assumed.',
      b: '32 kHz 16-bit stereo wav, so budget an audio super-resolution pass before release.',
      winner: 'a',
    },
    {
      dimension: 'Editing what you made',
      a: 'region editing, song extend, remix, and lyrics video, so an almost-right take gets fixed rather than rerolled.',
      b: 'regenerate, or adjust the prompt. there is no region editing, so almost-right usually means another pass.',
      winner: 'a',
    },
    {
      dimension: 'Rights clarity',
      a: 'commercial use on paid plans, with training data not disclosed.',
      b: 'a custom community licence to read against your use, with training data not disclosed.',
      winner: 'tie',
    },
  ]}
  narrative={[
    {
      heading: 'Buy the toolkit or build it',
      body: `<p>mureka's advantage is not the model, it is the ten things wrapped around it. separation into 5 or 12 stems, midi export, region editing, extend, remix, image and video soundtrack generation, fine-tuning, all behind one api. if you tried to assemble that around a raw checkpoint you would spend months and end up with something worse.</p><p>minimax's advantage is that the checkpoint is <strong>yours</strong>. no rate limit, no price rise, no service notice, no dependency on a company continuing to care about your use case. those are both real advantages and you cannot have both, which is what makes this an actual decision rather than a ranking.</p>`,
    },
    {
      heading: 'Count the whole pipeline before you compare cost',
      body: `<p>minimax looks cheaper because the weights are free. then you add gpu capacity for an 8B global model paired with a 0.6B local one, an audio super-resolution stage to get above 32 kHz, and whatever you build to replace stems, midi, and region editing, because none of those exist.</p><p>mureka looks more expensive because there is a bill. the bill covers all of the above, already working. <strong>the honest comparison is not weights against subscription, it is your total pipeline against theirs</strong>, and the answer flips depending on how much of that toolkit you actually need.</p>`,
    },
    {
      heading: 'Separation is a repair, not a source',
      body: `<p>worth saying plainly since it is mureka's headline feature. 5-stem and 12-stem separation pulls parts back out of a finished mix. it works well, and on dense material it leaves artefacts, because the information was genuinely lost when those parts were summed together.</p><p>that is a real limitation of every separation tool, not a mureka flaw. the way around it is not a better separator, it is not mixing the parts together in the first place. that is a different product shape, and it is the one producers keep asking for.</p>`,
    },
  ]}
  chooseA={[
    'need stems, midi, and region editing without building any of it',
    'are calling music generation from your own software today',
    'want to fine-tune a house sound through an api rather than a research pipeline',
    'need production-spec audio with no extra post-processing stage',
    'would rather pay a bill than run infrastructure',
  ]}
  chooseB={[
    'want the weights permanently, with no vendor in the loop',
    'generate at volume where fixed gpu cost beats per-call pricing',
    'need five minutes of song conceived as one take',
    'have the team to run inference and add audio super-resolution',
    'are building something that has to keep working regardless of anyone\'s roadmap',
  ]}
  faqs={[
    {
      question: 'Is Mureka or MiniMax Music 3 better for building a product?',
      answer: 'mureka, for most teams. its api already covers generation, extend, region editing, stem extraction, and fine-tuning, so you are integrating rather than building. minimax gives you an open checkpoint and nothing around it, which is the right choice only if owning the weights matters more than the six months of toolkit you would have to replace.',
    },
    {
      question: 'Does MiniMax Music 3 export stems?',
      answer: 'no. it returns a finished 32 kHz 16-bit stereo mix. mureka offers 5-stem and 12-stem separation on uploaded tracks. if you want parts that were never mixed together in the first place, sonura generates loops, one-shots, and stems separately on every paid plan.',
    },
    {
      question: 'Which is cheaper?',
      answer: 'it depends on volume and on how much of mureka\'s toolkit you need. minimax weights are free but you pay for gpu, for an audio super-resolution stage above 32 kHz, and for anything you build to replace stems, midi, and region editing. mureka has a bill that already covers all of that. compare whole pipelines, not headline prices.',
    },
    {
      question: 'Can I fine-tune either one?',
      answer: 'both, differently. mureka exposes a fine-tuning api, so a custom model can be trained, versioned, and called from your code. minimax publishes open weights, so you can fine-tune however you want and you are building that pipeline yourself. mureka is faster to a result, minimax gives you more control.',
    },
    {
      question: 'What licence does MiniMax Music 3 use?',
      answer: 'a custom community licence rather than a standard open one, and the training data is not disclosed. read it against your specific use before releasing commercially. mureka grants commercial use on paid plans, also without disclosing training data.',
    },
    {
      question: 'How good is Mureka stem separation?',
      answer: 'solid, and reported as on par with dedicated separation tools, in 5-stem and 12-stem variants. it is still separation, which reconstructs parts from a finished mix and leaves artefacts on dense material. generating parts separately avoids that entirely.',
    },
  ]}
/>
